{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XhpBvL7NfBC2kuaMysTFEn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n4\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n5\t    \"line\": 97,\n6\t    \"comment\": \"The delayed trigger is registered with event_type=TriggeredEvent (the base class) and condition=None, which means it will fire on ANY TriggeredEvent the engine fires -- not just 'at the beginning of your next main phase' as the oracle text specifies. In actual gameplay (via TriggerManager.fire_event), this trigger would match every EntersBattlefieldTriggeredEvent, SpellCastTriggeredEvent, DealsDamageTriggeredEvent, etc. The tests pass only because they call trigger.effect(game) directly rather than going through fire_event. A correct implementation should either (a) use an appropriate event_type like a BeginningOfMainPhaseTriggeredEvent with a condition checking the controller is the active player, or (b) at minimum use a more specific event type with a condition that restricts firing to the intended timing.\"\n7\t  },\n8\t  {\n9\t    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n10\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n11\t    \"line\": 97,\n12\t    \"comment\": \"The trigger is never unregistered after firing. Delayed triggers in MTG are one-shot: they fire once and are consumed. Since condition=None and event_type=TriggeredEvent, this trigger will fire on every single event indefinitely. The effect closure should unregister itself from the trigger_manager after executing, e.g. by calling game.trigger_manager.unregister(source) inside _effect, or wrapping it to auto-remove after the first invocation.\"\n13\t  },\n14\t  {\n15\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n16\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n17\t    \"line\": 79,\n18\t    \"comment\": \"The counter logic manually removes the spell from spell_owner.zones[Zone.STACK] and adds it to spell_owner.zones[Zone.GRAVEYARD], but does not remove the corresponding StackObject from game.stack. If the countered spell's StackObject is still on the stack, the priority_loop will eventually pop and resolve it (calling its on_resolve), even though the card has already been moved to the graveyard. In a real game integration this would cause double-resolution or errors. Consider also removing the StackObject from game.stack (there is no direct removal API, but this is worth noting as an engine limitation).\"\n19\t  },\n20\t  {\n21\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n22\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n23\t    \"line\": 73,\n24\t    \"comment\": \"The oracle text says 'equal to the amount of mana spent to cast that spell' which refers to actual mana spent (which can differ from CMC due to cost reductions, additional costs, or X spells). The implementation uses mana_cost.cmc as a proxy. The rationale acknowledges this as a simplification, and the engine does not currently track actual mana spent per spell, so this is acceptable for now. However, if the engine later gains mana-spent tracking (e.g. via a field on StackObject), this should be updated.\"\n25\t  }\n26\t]\n27\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1fe34661-6a3c-4b16-8a42-0f618466dfcb","uuid":"eb9f55c3-363c-4a1b-8e32-47a1a889be9a","timestamp":"2026-05-28T09:45:18.808Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_57/review.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 97,\n    \"comment\": \"The delayed trigger is registered with event_type=TriggeredEvent (the base class) and condition=None, which means it will fire on ANY TriggeredEvent the engine fires -- not just 'at the beginning of your next main phase' as the oracle text specifies. In actual gameplay (via TriggerManager.fire_event), this trigger would match every EntersBattlefieldTriggeredEvent, SpellCastTriggeredEvent, DealsDamageTriggeredEvent, etc. The tests pass only because they call trigger.effect(game) directly rather than going through fire_event. A correct implementation should either (a) use an appropriate event_type like a BeginningOfMainPhaseTriggeredEvent with a condition checking the controller is the active player, or (b) at minimum use a more specific event type with a condition that restricts firing to the intended timing.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 97,\n    \"comment\": \"The trigger is never unregistered after firing. Delayed triggers in MTG are one-shot: they fire once and are consumed. Since condition=None and event_type=TriggeredEvent, this trigger will fire on every single event indefinitely. The effect closure should unregister itself from the trigger_manager after executing, e.g. by calling game.trigger_manager.unregister(source) inside _effect, or wrapping it to auto-remove after the first invocation.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 79,\n    \"comment\": \"The counter logic manually removes the spell from spell_owner.zones[Zone.STACK] and adds it to spell_owner.zones[Zone.GRAVEYARD], but does not remove the corresponding StackObject from game.stack. If the countered spell's StackObject is still on the stack, the priority_loop will eventually pop and resolve it (calling its on_resolve), even though the card has already been moved to the graveyard. In a real game integration this would cause double-resolution or errors. Consider also removing the StackObject from game.stack (there is no direct removal API, but this is worth noting as an engine limitation).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 73,\n    \"comment\": \"The oracle text says 'equal to the amount of mana spent to cast that spell' which refers to actual mana spent (which can differ from CMC due to cost reductions, additional costs, or X spells). The implementation uses mana_cost.cmc as a proxy. The rationale acknowledges this as a simplification, and the engine does not currently track actual mana spent per spell, so this is acceptable for now. However, if the engine later gains mana-spent tracking (e.g. via a field on StackObject), this should be updated.\"\n  }\n]\n","numLines":27,"startLine":1,"totalLines":27}}}
