{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NAyvBq6aDYXsVjGLjHr3d9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R4yvVA3bxMuAGakExAd2vC","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt","offset":5530,"limit":15},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":807,"cache_read_input_tokens":53810,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":807,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":72,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PXaXZnxyVjH8ZnnttBcNNj","session_id":"1fe34661-6a3c-4b16-8a42-0f618466dfcb","uuid":"a1c6cf47-faff-47e3-86df-2331a55aab4a","request_id":"req_011CbUkpnweak9eKx52HcBWc","subagent_type":"Tester","task_description":"Write tests for sos_97"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R4yvVA3bxMuAGakExAd2vC","type":"tool_result","content":"5530\tExample: You control Young Wolf, a 1/1 creature with undying, and it has a +1/+1 counter on it. A spell puts three -1/-1 counters on Young Wolf. Before state-based actions are performed, Young Wolf has one +1/+1 counter and three -1/-1 counters on it. After state-based actions are performed, Young Wolf is in the graveyard. When it was last on the battlefield, it had a +1/+1 counter on it, so undying will not trigger.\n5531\t\n5532\t705. Flipping a Coin\n5533\t\n5534\t705.1. Some cards refer to flipping a coin. A coin used in a flip must be a two-sided object with easily distinguished sides and equal likelihood that either side lands face up. If the coin that’s being flipped doesn’t have an obvious “heads” or “tails,” designate one side to be “heads,” and the other side to be “tails.” Other methods of randomization may be substituted for flipping a coin as long as there are two possible outcomes of equal likelihood and all players agree to the substitution. For example, the player may roll an even-sided die and call “odds” or “evens,” or roll an even-sided die and designate that “odds” means “heads” and “evens” means “tails.”\n5535\t\n5536\t705.2. Some effects that instruct a player to flip a coin care only about whether the coin comes up heads or tails. No player wins or loses a coin flip for this kind of effect. For all other effects that instruct a player to flip a coin, the player that flips the coin calls “heads” or “tails.” If the call matches the result, the player wins the flip. Otherwise, the player loses the flip. Only the player who flips the coin wins or loses the flip; no other players are involved.\n5537\t\n5538\t705.3. An effect may state that a coin flip has a certain result and/or that a certain player wins a coin flip. In that case, ignore the actual results of that flip and use the indicated results instead. This can cause a player to win a flip that couldn’t otherwise be won.\n5539\t\n5540\t706. Rolling a Die\n5541\t\n5542\t706.1. An effect that instructs a player to roll a die will specify what kind of die to roll and how many of those dice to roll.\n5543\t\n5544\t706.1a Such an effect may refer to an “N-sided die,” “N-sided dice,” or one or more “dN,” where N is a positive integer. In those cases, the die must have N equally likely outcomes, numbered from 1 to N. For example, a d20 is a twenty-sided die with possible outcomes from 1 to 20."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PXaXZnxyVjH8ZnnttBcNNj","session_id":"1fe34661-6a3c-4b16-8a42-0f618466dfcb","uuid":"40bed8a6-1fed-45ad-b9e0-d7ced5f54553","timestamp":"2026-05-28T09:48:22.349Z","subagent_type":"Tester","task_description":"Write tests for sos_97"}
