{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WozL6pj9MawmY4HRgLf1k4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n4\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n5\t    \"line\": 73,\n6\t    \"comment\": \"Race condition with shared mutable `last_event` dict: The condition closure captures the triggering spell into `last_event[\\\"spell\\\"]`, but the effect closure reads it later on resolution. If the controller casts two instants/sorceries before the first trigger resolves (e.g., responds with a second instant), the second spell overwrites `last_event[\\\"spell\\\"]`. When the first trigger resolves (LIFO -- the one for the first spell), it will incorrectly copy the second spell instead of the first. Fix: capture the spell reference at condition-evaluation time in a way that is scoped per-trigger-firing, not shared across all firings. For example, bind the spell into the StackObject at push time by generating a new closure per fire, or store per-StackObject data.\"\n7\t  },\n8\t  {\n9\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n10\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n11\t    \"line\": 137,\n12\t    \"comment\": \"The return value of `controller.choose_yes_no(\\\"Choose new targets for the copy?\\\")` is discarded. The player is asked whether to choose new targets but the answer has no effect -- no target re-selection logic follows. This makes the choose_yes_no call a no-op that still consumes a scripted answer in tests. Consider either implementing target re-selection or removing this call.\"\n13\t  },\n14\t  {\n15\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n16\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n17\t    \"line\": 82,\n18\t    \"comment\": \"Rules deviation: Casualty is defined as an additional cost paid as part of casting (CR 702.153), not a triggered ability. The current implementation models it as a triggered ability that fires on SpellCastTriggeredEvent and resolves later. This means: (1) the sacrifice happens during trigger resolution rather than during casting, so the sacrificed creature could be removed before the trigger resolves; (2) the opponent can respond between the spell being cast and the casualty being paid. For a medium-complexity card this may be an acceptable simplification, but it deviates from Oracle text behavior.\"\n19\t  },\n20\t  {\n21\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n22\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n23\t    \"line\": 100,\n24\t    \"comment\": \"Minor: `getattr(obj, \\\"power\\\", 0)` works correctly for Creature instances (which have a `power` property), but the fallback of 0 could mask issues on non-Creature objects that happen to have card_types containing CREATURE without a power attribute. This is unlikely in practice given the engine's class hierarchy.\"\n25\t  }\n26\t]\n27\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1fe34661-6a3c-4b16-8a42-0f618466dfcb","uuid":"2de6bd64-3bc2-40e4-bb65-622d6795aef1","timestamp":"2026-05-28T10:22:59.774Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_226/review.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 73,\n    \"comment\": \"Race condition with shared mutable `last_event` dict: The condition closure captures the triggering spell into `last_event[\\\"spell\\\"]`, but the effect closure reads it later on resolution. If the controller casts two instants/sorceries before the first trigger resolves (e.g., responds with a second instant), the second spell overwrites `last_event[\\\"spell\\\"]`. When the first trigger resolves (LIFO -- the one for the first spell), it will incorrectly copy the second spell instead of the first. Fix: capture the spell reference at condition-evaluation time in a way that is scoped per-trigger-firing, not shared across all firings. For example, bind the spell into the StackObject at push time by generating a new closure per fire, or store per-StackObject data.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 137,\n    \"comment\": \"The return value of `controller.choose_yes_no(\\\"Choose new targets for the copy?\\\")` is discarded. The player is asked whether to choose new targets but the answer has no effect -- no target re-selection logic follows. This makes the choose_yes_no call a no-op that still consumes a scripted answer in tests. Consider either implementing target re-selection or removing this call.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 82,\n    \"comment\": \"Rules deviation: Casualty is defined as an additional cost paid as part of casting (CR 702.153), not a triggered ability. The current implementation models it as a triggered ability that fires on SpellCastTriggeredEvent and resolves later. This means: (1) the sacrifice happens during trigger resolution rather than during casting, so the sacrificed creature could be removed before the trigger resolves; (2) the opponent can respond between the spell being cast and the casualty being paid. For a medium-complexity card this may be an acceptable simplification, but it deviates from Oracle text behavior.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 100,\n    \"comment\": \"Minor: `getattr(obj, \\\"power\\\", 0)` works correctly for Creature instances (which have a `power` property), but the fallback of 0 could mask issues on non-Creature objects that happen to have card_types containing CREATURE without a power attribute. This is unlikely in practice given the engine's class hierarchy.\"\n  }\n]\n","numLines":27,"startLine":1,"totalLines":27}}}
